de to take the colony and  read arrive it or start a new colony and  you only select a handful of animals to  do that in the end your population that  you're starting with and the third  figure here is going to have far more  red than white and in the end you may  end up bringing the white out completely  so this is what genetic bottlenecking is  and what you have to do when you start a  new population or you're maintaining an  out read to keep that change from  happening you need to maintain an  appropriate size a colony size and that  would be 25 unrelated pair or more so to  minimize genetic change you need to  maintain the appropriate number of  animals in your colony avoid selection  pressure you need to follow a specific  breeding protocol have excellent record  keeping monitor your colonies and just  know what you you know know what you're  starting with and know what you want to  shoot for in the end so the last part of  this talk I really just wanted to do a  few case studies on how genetic  background matters I think I've run into  some people that have thought that you  know all that matters is the mutation so  it doesn't matter what genetic  background it on the animal is that  whatever mutation it has will cause the  phenotype that's not always the case for  example we have the LEP rdb model or the  diabetic mouse and it is a model of type  1 diabetes it's a spot  kenya's single nucleotide mutation in  the lepr gene and what it causes is OB  obesity and the early onset of juvenile  hyper gleich hyperglycemia it you lose  me up there's a massive loss of beta  cells and islet atrophy within two  months after puberty and this is on the  c57 black Kahless inbred strain it  causes obesity and transient diabetes on  the black six inbred strain so what you  can see is you have a much different  phenotype on a Kahless background then  you do on a black six background so it's  much more it's much more severe on a  Kahless than it is on a black six the  skin model is another good example this  is severe combined immunodeficiency and  on an odd genetic background it lacks T  cells B cells severe NK cell impairment  there's no compliment activity there's  impaired macrophage development and  impaired antigen presenting cell  function on a bob c background it only  lacks t-cells and b-cells but all the  other cells work so that's there's a  very big difference and I know skids one  of those where I think sometimes people  think you know it's such a severe  mutation it doesn't matter what  background is on but it definitely  matters and your animal is going to be  more immuno compromised on an odd  background and on a bouncy background  they may be surprised at the result the  last example is um what I won't you know  spill it all the letters here but this  is a g93a transgenic mouse model and it  contains several mutant human sonic 1  genes and it's used in the study of ALS  you get severe progressive  neurodegeneration of the motor system  when you use sjl and c3h inbred mouse  models but the phenotype is much milder  if you were to use a black 6 a black 10  or about c model so there's a big  difference there in the phenotype  also if it's on a mixed background the  phenotype is much milder so in summary  you should know the genetic background  of the model that you're working with  minimize genetic change in your colony  once you know what you have know what  you know what you're shooting for so you  need to minimize genet you need a  midnight minimize genetic drift you need  to not have genetic contamination if at  all possible but if you do you need a  way to be able to detect that remember  that genetic background can be a very  large research fear variable and that's  the end of the talk thank you thank you  both for your informative presentations  it's time for Q&amp;A if you have a question  you'd like to ask Mandy horn or sherry  wild please do so now just click on the  Queen Q&amp;A button at the lower left of  the presentation window type your  question into the box it appears on your  screen and click the send button will  answer as many questions as we have time  for let's get started our first question  is do you have or know of a checklist to  help in an investigation when problems  are found as in the pregnancy resorption  example so that's a really good question  we don't have a checklist per se you  know within our department at NV go we  provide technical support and so  something that we do is you know like I  alluded to with this presentation was we  have a list of items that will go  through so you know we'll look at you  know we'll start with husband durian and  changes to animal technicians will look  at the animal model maybe even the  genetics or the consistency of the  animals that are coming out will  consider the bedding in the diet you  know we'll look at temperature and  humidity you know differences to cage  density or construction or noise so you  know the the one slide you know that  listed all the different parameters  that's that's kind of a good checklist  I'd say I'm worried a good place to  start and really you know I think as you  start to investigate these things you  know you'll start to go through and one  item may lead to another and and so you  may you know start looking at the animal  technician and then recognize that the  betting changed and then recognize that  the cage density changed and you may  find a lot of different parameter  changes as you go through it how do you  deal when the control key deal when the  control group has the same background  but is not related to the GM strain the  control group came from somewhere else  um you know is that I'm sorry it won't  work okay sorry I think that's a that's  actually a really good question um it's  difficult because doing genetic  monitoring to check your control strain  the technology is there there are panels  that you can use and I would suggest you  can use panels that are a little bit  deeper that are going to give you a  little bit more resolution to check and  and you can compare the backgrounds of  your your your transgenic strain to your  control strain but it doesn't really  give you the whole picture it can tell  you if there are large changes in  between the two models but you would  have to sequence the entire genome of  both animals to tell if there are a  hundred percent the same so my advice to  people is always to try to get a control  that comes from the same place as the  background of the other model but you  can also use I mean if you talk about  vendors for example you can also use  them as a resource because we have a lot  of information about the genetic  background of the different substrains  so for example if you have a transgenic  model that was on a you know a black 6j  model from jackson and you want to know  if you can use an n' as your control  that's a that's  thing that we could give you information  on and tell you what sort of things to  expect what sort of genetic differences  there are but there's not really a  hundred percent great answer for that  one it is it is one that you struggle  with but I definitely think you should  run at least some of the larger panels  on some of those we have panels that are  1,500 markers that are routinely run but  at least give you some idea that you're  in the ballpark with your control strain  the next person says this is a question  more about environment than genetics is  there any data or inference that the  various methods of rodent ID ear tags  punches chips tattoos might impact the  animal and study data that's a good  question I'm not aware of any specifics  I would think that there could be some  just looking at different levels of  stress that a different you know that a  different identification method might  have you know maybe you know one would  create more acute stress than another  one may lead to more chronic stress as  the animals healing and so certainly an  acclimation period around the ID you  know the identification time period  might might be recommended but that's  you know unfortunately that's not  something I'm aware of in the literature  but I think it's an excellent question  and something to consider you know as  part of your study if there are no more  questions that would like to once again  thank Mandy horn and sherry wild for  their presentations do you have any  final comments to share with us today no  they thank you again for the opportunity  and and you know if there are questions  afterwards you know we'll certainly be  able to take those  thank you as a reminder this  presentation has been approved for  continuing educational credits if you  want to obtain the credits please click  on the get your free secrets button  located in the lower left of your screen  this will take you to a page listing all  of our speakers and presentations please  select the c/e button under the  presentation and follow the instructions  to claim your certificate before I go I  want to let everyone know that today's  webcast will be available for on-demand  viewing through august 3rd 2016 you'll  receive an email from lab roots letting  you know when this webcast will be  available for replay please show that  announcement with your colleagues who  may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us we hope to see you again soon bye
 

